detail.abstract:
This study proposes a mathematical framework for explicitly describing the structural generation mechanism of implied volatility (IV). While IV has traditionally been treated primarily as a forecasting target, its internal structure and underlying dynamics remain insufficiently understood. In this paper, we introduce a state variable based on the relationship between realized variance (RV) and IV, and formulate its dynamics as a Fokker-Planck-type stochastic process. Specifically, we define the state variable as&nbsp;
<div>
 x t = log RV t IV t&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 and assume that it follows a mean-reverting stochastic process with state-dependent diffusion. Using data from the U.S. equity market (SPY) and the VIX index, we estimate the model parameters and examine the consistency between the theoretically derived stationary distribution and the empirical distribution. The results indicate that the proposed framework successfully captures the fundamental dynamics governing the relationship between IV and RV.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Furthermore, the proposed model enables a structural decomposition of IV into a systematic component and a residual component. More precisely, the observed market IV can be interpreted as the sum of a structural volatility component implied by the RV-IV dynamics and an additional deviation corresponding to a dynamic risk premium. This deviation reflects market sentiment, supply-demand imbalances, and nonlinear risk perceptions.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings suggest that IV should not be regarded as a purely exogenous market indicator, but rather as an endogenous variable that can be structurally reconstructed from pricebased variance dynamics. The proposed framework provides a new theoretical foundation for understanding volatility dynamics and offers potential applications in risk management, volatility modeling, and dynamic asset allocation.
</div>
